# Personal Development Rules

## Skills

Read the matching skill immediately when the task starts:

- `markus-investigate` — debugging, root-cause, explaining existing behavior, proposing a fix
- `markus-code-design` — writing, editing, refactoring, or designing application code
- `markus-dense-prose` — commit messages, PR titles/bodies, Jira tickets/comments, investigation or report docs
- `markus-git-commit` — committing, arranging commits, branch naming, pushing
- `markus-pull-request` — opening or updating PRs, review comments, GitHub read vs write

## Mindset

Every conclusion must be demonstrated with evidence from code or logs. Comments, PRs, docs, and my assertions are circumstantial. Cite as `path:LINE` or `path:START-END`. Think aloud; don't just present conclusions. Stop on unexpected scope expansion. Gate complex unsolicited plans.

### Correct mistakes silently; don't narrate them

When I point out a mistake, or you catch your own, fix it in place and move on. The corrected
artifact—document, code, comment, commit message—should read as if the mistake was never there.
Not every mistake warrants an explanation: usually correcting it is enough. Don't add a note about
what was wrong, what you removed or renamed, or that a correction happened; that narration is
clutter that outlives its usefulness and misleads later readers. The "before" state lives in git
history, which is where it belongs. If a correction genuinely needs explaining, explain it to me in
chat, not in the artifact.

## Command Output Discipline

Treat command output as expensive. Don't truncate with `head`/`tail` directly - use `tee` to save
to a file first so you can inspect again if needed.

## Tooling & Specific Commands

### Git

For read-only git (inspecting history, diffs, status, branches, config, etc.), prefer `git-ro`
instead of `git`. It is an allowlisted wrapper that runs only read-only git and refuses anything
that can write, so it is safe to auto-run without confirmation. Use the same arguments as `git`
(e.g. `git-ro log --oneline`, `git-ro branch --contains HEAD`, `git-ro config --get remote.origin.url`).
Fall back to plain `git` only for operations that write (commit, add, checkout, rebase, push, …).

I often work in ongoing rebases, moving to earlier commits to fix or add things. Always orient
yourself first - check `git status` to see if a rebase is in progress.

### Buildkite CI Logs on Failure

```bash
# List builds for a branch
bk build list --pipeline PIPELINE_NAME --branch YOUR_BRANCH_NAME

# View build details (jobs, state, metadata)
bk build view --pipeline PIPELINE_NAME BUILD_NUMBER
bk build view --pipeline PIPELINE_NAME BUILD_NUMBER | jq '.jobs[] | {name, state}'

# Find failed jobs
bk build view --pipeline PIPELINE_NAME BUILD_NUMBER | jq '.jobs[] | select(.state == "failed")'

# Get job log (JOB_ID from build view output)
bk api "/pipelines/PIPELINE_NAME/builds/BUILD_NUMBER/jobs/JOB_ID/log" | jq -r '.content' | tail -100

# List artifacts (note: build number is positional, not a flag)
bk artifacts list BUILD_NUMBER --pipeline PIPELINE_NAME

# Download artifact by UUID
bk artifacts download ARTIFACT_UUID
```

### Jira

Jira status is not authoritative—tickets may be stale or incomplete. The code is the only source of
truth. Use Jira for context and history, but verify claims in code before acting on them.

Use the Atlassian MCP when it has a matching tool. For tools that write prose to Jira, put the prose
into a temporary markdown file so that I can review it. Fall back to `acli jira` when necessary.

Use **`acli jira`** (subcommands under `acli jira workitem`). There is no `acli jira issue`.

When searching issues, don't limit results arbitrarily (e.g., `head -20`). The full list is usually
manageable and missing tickets means missing context.

**View work item (default fields: key, issuetype, summary, status, assignee, description):**
```bash
acli jira workitem view TICKET-1234
# With description explicitly (or when ticket has no description, request it)
acli jira workitem view TICKET-1234 --fields "key,summary,description"
# All fields
acli jira workitem view TICKET-1234 --fields "*all"
```

**Comments:** `acli jira workitem comment list --key TICKET-1234` truncates multi-line comments to
the first paragraph. To get full comment bodies, use the workitem view endpoint with the `comment`
field and JSON output:
```bash
acli jira workitem view TICKET-1234 --fields comment --json
```
The bodies are in Atlassian Document Format (ADF)—a nested JSON structure with node types like
`paragraph`, `orderedList`, `bulletList`, `listItem`, `text`, `mention`, `hardBreak`.

**Search:**
```bash
acli jira workitem search --jql "project = PROJECT AND text ~ 'marketplace'" --limit 30 --fields "key,summary,status"
```

### Tool Selection

Prefer narrower-scope tools over general-purpose ones where feasible. A focused tool states intent
more clearly and is lighter weight than reaching for a programming language.

### ripgrep (rg)

Always run `rg` with a closed/unreadable stdin (e.g. `rg PATTERN </dev/null`), or pass an explicit
path to search (e.g. `rg PATTERN .`). With no path and a readable stdin, rg searches stdin instead
of the file system. The agent shell connects stdin to a socket, which rg counts as readable, so a
bare `rg PATTERN` hangs forever reading a socket that never delivers data — it never touches the
file system.

### JSON parsing

Use jq to parse JSON on the command line. Avoid using python for that.

### Google Docs

Use `gdoc-ro` (read-only, safe) and `gdoc-rw` (writes) - they auth via gcloud.
Run `gdoc-ro help` / `gdoc-rw help` for the full surface. Both accept a doc URL or bare ID.

```bash
gdoc-ro info  https://docs.google.com/document/d/DOC_ID/edit
gdoc-ro export DOC_ID --mime text/markdown > doc.md      # also: text/plain, text/html, application/pdf
gdoc-ro read DOC_ID | jq '.body.content[-1].endIndex'    # find indices for inserts

gdoc-rw append DOC_ID "- new bullet"
echo "from stdin" | gdoc-rw append DOC_ID
gdoc-rw replace DOC_ID "TODO" "DONE"                      # add -i for case-insensitive
gdoc-rw delete-range DOC_ID START END                     # half-open [start, end)
gdoc-rw create "New doc title" --from-stdin < draft.md
gdoc-rw create "New doc title" --from-stdin --markdown < draft.md
                                                          # converts MD to formatted Doc (headings,
                                                          # lists, code, links) via Drive import.
gdoc-rw replace-content DOC_ID --markdown < draft.md      # rewrite an existing doc from markdown,
                                                          # preserving the URL.
gdoc-rw batch DOC_ID < requests.json                      # raw documents.batchUpdate requests
```

To skip the per-call `gcloud` invocation, cache the token in the environment:
`export GDOC_TOKEN=$(gdoc-ro token)`.

Docs API indices are character offsets in the body; index 1 is the body start; the last
segment's endIndex is one past the final character (insert at endIndex - 1); ranges are
half-open. To do anything beyond the high-level subcommands, inspect indices with
`gdoc-ro read` and feed JSON requests to `gdoc-rw batch`.
